WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.900
Load balancing.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:07.633
A load balancer spreads traffic across your replicas, and quietly removes the ones that die.

3
00:00:08.833 --> 00:00:11.567
A load balancer sits in front of your instances.

4
00:00:11.867 --> 00:00:13.433
Clients send requests to it.

5
00:00:13.733 --> 00:00:15.400
It picks a backend for each one.

6
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:19.600
This works because the replicas are stateless, so any of them can answer.

7
00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:23.000
Three strategies cover most interviews.

8
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.240
Round robin simply takes turns.

9
00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:26.367
It is the default.

10
00:00:26.667 --> 00:00:31.408
Least connections sends work to the instance with the fewest open requests.

11
00:00:31.408 --> 00:00:35.833
That is better when durations vary wildly, like streaming LLM answers.

12
00:00:36.133 --> 00:00:43.133
Consistent hashing sends the same key to the same instance, and adding a node reshuffles only a sliver of keys.

13
00:00:44.333 --> 00:00:46.567
The production-grade part is health checks.

14
00:00:46.867 --> 00:00:51.300
The balancer probes each backend, with a health ping or by counting failures.

15
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.267
Dead backends leave rotation automatically.

16
00:00:54.567 --> 00:00:56.667
When they recover, they come back.

17
00:00:56.667 --> 00:01:00.867
Deploys, crashes, and out of memory kills become invisible to users.

18
00:01:02.067 --> 00:01:03.867
Here is the Nginx config.

19
00:01:04.167 --> 00:01:08.600
We choose least connections, because LLM streams have wild durations.

20
00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:12.433
After two failures, a backend is ejected for ten seconds.

21
00:01:12.733 --> 00:01:17.133
And if a backend errors mid request, Nginx replays it on the next one.

22
00:01:18.333 --> 00:01:21.222
Two replicas of the RAG API sit behind Nginx.

23
00:01:21.222 --> 00:01:30.467
Round robin starved one of them, because streamed answers hold connections for twenty seconds while health queries finish in fifty milliseconds.

24
00:01:30.767 --> 00:01:36.000
To prove it works, run a five hundred request load test and kill api two halfway.

25
00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:45.300
The dead backend is ejected within a second, the in-flight failures are replayed on api one, and the test finishes with zero failed requests.

26
00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:47.257
Let's recap.

27
00:01:47.257 --> 00:01:50.600
A balancer spreads traffic across stateless replicas.

28
00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:54.333
Pick round robin, least connections, or consistent hashing.

29
00:01:54.633 --> 00:01:56.600
Health checks remove dead backends.

30
00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:58.885
And prove it with a kill test.

31
00:01:58.885 --> 00:02:01.267
Next up: distributed systems basics.
